neutron stars in the universe tend to contain matter of supranuclear density in their interiors , with typical mass @xmath0 and radii @xmath1 km . as one class of compact objects , neutron stars have been arousing tremendous interest amongst scientists because of many novel features . one of the features of neutron stars is their strong magnetic field that could be the largest one observed in nature . the typical magnitudes of a surface magnetic fields are as large as @xmath2 g @xcite . it is currently assumed that the soft gamma repeaters ( sgr ) and anomalous x - ray pulsars , candidates for the magnetars , have a strong surface magnetic fields up to @xmath3 g @xcite . the magnetic field in the interior could be as large as @xmath4 g according to the scalar virial theorem @xcite . it is interesting that the strong magnetic fields were also created in heavy - ion collisions @xcite , which may help us to understand the response of the dense matter under the presence of strong magnetic fields .    over the past decades , many works have been dedicated to the effects of the magnetic field on neutron star properties , such as the equation of states @xcite , neutron star structure @xcite , transport properties and the cooling or heating of magnetized stars @xcite . an unclear but interesting problem is the origin of such strong magnetic field . a simple analysis showed that a weak magnetic field in a progenitor star could be amplified during the gravitational collapse due to magnetic flux conservation . however , it can not explain the very strong surface magnetic field in magnetars @xcite . another explanation called the magnetohydrodynamic dynamo mechanism based on the rapidly rotating plasma of a protoneutron star @xcite which is generally accepted as the standard explanation for the origin of the magnetar s large magnetic fields , is unable to explain all the features of the supernova remnants surrounding these objects @xcite . an interesting mechanism being suggested for the origin is the possible existence of a phase transition to a ferromagnetic state , namely spontaneous magnetization . such argument has been investigated widely within various theoretical approaches ( without the background magnetic field ) @xcite , but the results are still divergent . even some authors showed that a possibility that a strong magnetic field is produced by color ferromagnetic quark matter in neutron stars @xcite . astronomical observations found that the sgr 1806 - 20 emitted a giant flare on 27 december 2004 with the total flare energy by @xmath5 erg and the energy release probably occurred during a catastrophic reconfiguration of the neutron star s magnetic field since the emitted energy significantly exceeds the rotational energy loss in the same period @xcite . these phenomena are perhaps related to the magnetization of the neutron star matter . in addition , the anisotropic pressure is related to the magnetization for the magnetized matter @xcite . therefore , the magnetization is an important physical quantity for neutron stars . some calculations have been performed for the magnetization of nuclear matter or pure neutron matter in magnetic fields @xcite . seldom calculations were carried out for the @xmath6-stable matter . in ref . @xcite , the magnetization of the @xmath6-stable matter was studied and it is shown that the magnetization never appears to become very large . however , this conclusion could be revised according to our calculations , as shown later . because of the small mass and hence the small magneton , the magnetization of electrons may be important compared with that of neutron . therefore , in the present study , the magnetization of the @xmath6-stable neutron star matter , which consists of protons , neutrons , electrons and muons , will be investigated . not only the amm of nucleons but also the one of leptons are included here . the main purposes of the this study are as follows . firstly , the contribution of each component as well as the effect of the anomalous magnetic moments ( amm ) will be analyzed in detail . secondly , we further explore whether the strong magnetic fields of the neutron stars originate from the highly degenerate relativistic electron gas . finally , the symmetry energy effects on the magnetization will be presented . this work is organized as follows . in sec . ii , a brief introduction of the relativistic mean field approach is presented . the magnetization of each component of the neutron star matter , along with the effects of the amm and the symmetry energy , are analyzed in detail in sec . finally a summary is given in sec . nowadays the relativistic mean field ( rmf ) theory as a density - functional approach has become a very useful tool in nuclear physics @xcite . in the rmf theory of nuclear matter that made of nucleons ( p , n ) and leptons ( e , @xmath7 ) in a uniform magnetic field @xmath8 , the total interacting lagrangian density is given by @xmath9 with @xmath10 and @xmath11 $ ] . @xmath12 , @xmath13 , @xmath14 and @xmath15 are the amm for protons , neutrons , electrons and muons , respectively  @xcite , where @xmath16 ( @xmath17 ) denotes the nuclear ( bohr ) magneton of nucleons ( leptons ) . @xmath18 , @xmath19 , @xmath20 and @xmath21 are the nucleon- , the @xmath22- , the @xmath23- and the @xmath24-meson masses , respectively . the nucleon field @xmath25 interacts with the @xmath26 meson fields @xmath27 and with the photon field @xmath28 . the field tensors for the vector meson are given as @xmath29 and by similar expression for @xmath24 meson and the photon . the self - coupling terms with coupling constants @xmath30 and @xmath31 for the @xmath22 meson turned out to be crucial  @xcite are introduced . compared with the previous rmf models , the rmf interactions employed in this work are fsugold where two additional parameters @xmath32 and @xmath33 have been introduced : @xmath23 meson self - interactions as described by @xmath3